# cflow M5 (host H1): does the card's attention window leave the host DRAM idle long enough to prefetch the next layer's experts?
# (cflow audit host.md H1, E10; the lever's price is Delta = h x host DRAM share, +10 .. +24 % on V4.1 at h 0.2 .. 0.4.)
#
# Two quantities carry the price and they are different things. (1) the DRAM-idle fraction of the attention window: whether
# the memory system is free while the card runs A(l+1) [this card]. (2) the prefetch hit rate h: the share of the next
# layer's host-read expert bytes that W_router(l+1) . x_l predicts [the audit's h, assumed 0.2 .. 0.4, never measured]. No
# runner reads (2): it needs each layer's handoff x dumped and a Mac-side router product; the lever registry has no handoff dump
# for generate_ds41 (ROUTE_TRACE writes the routed ids, not x; crates/levers/src/registry.rs:683), so that item stops here.
# The task text asks for (1) from DRAM counters. The box has none [measured 2026-10-29 on ws, read-only probes]: kernel
# 6.8.0-147, perf 6.8.12, perf_event_paranoid 4 (root), `perf stat -a -M nps1_die_to_dram -- sleep 1` ends "Cannot find PMU
# `dram_channel_data_controller_4'. Missing kernel support?", and /sys/bus/event_source/devices lists amd_iommu_0-3, breakpoint,
# cpu, ibs_fetch, ibs_op, kprobe, msr, power, software, tracepoint, uprobe: no amd_df, amd_umc or amd_l3. The core PMU's
# ls_*_fills_from_sys.dram_io events count L1D fills only, miss a hardware-prefetched line the L2 already holds, and perf stat
# -I cannot cut below 10 ms against a 0.3 ms window; they bound nothing here. So (1) is derived, and the box measures its one
# open term, the window's width.
# Derivation [derived]. Host DRAM readers during the card's attention window A(l+1): the union's 32 threads spin on the go word
# (cache-resident, no DRAM); the PCIe DMA of x and the host sums, 5.85 MB a token [measured, rig-log log/2026-09-30.md:83] =
# 0.4 % of the 1.43 GB the union reads a step (11.1 ms x 128.4 GB/s, host.md 0.1); the residency swap's staged copy when
# residency is on, k = 3..4 passes of 30..60 MB a token (adaptres: 1.8-3.5 ms a token) = 0.09..0.24 GB, 6..17 % of the union's
# bytes; the engram and embedding rows, a few MB. So the DRAM-idle fraction in the window is 0.83..1.00, the low edge with
# residency on. The lever then needs the window to hold the next layer's bytes: host bytes a layer 36 MB (1.43 GB / 40) at the
# union's 128.4 GB/s is W = 0.28 ms, a step's worth 11.2 ms. The card segment is 12.96 ms a step = 0.324 ms a layer [measured
# 2026-09-24, stale: r8, ds41bulk router and the other card kernel changes since are not in it]: 16 % over W. Predicted now:
# 11..15 ms (the card chain's kernels moved by about -1 .. +2 ms).
# Resource timeline of one layer, V4.1 plan (a), decode (host.md 1): card SMs A(l) 0.32 ms, then host DRAM + 32 threads H(l) 0.28
# ms with the card on C(l) (shadow, under the union); PCIe ~0.005 ms a layer; NVMe 0. Wall = the card's chain + the host
# exposure, a sum: attention and union do not overlap inside a token; the lever overlaps the NEXT layer's reads (DRAM) under
# A(l+1). The step is 22.8 ms: window 12.96 + union 11.1 - overlap. A DRAM-side lever can recover at most min(A, W) a layer.
# Runner. nsys-ds41.sh's decode form (default place a, A6000, residency as the plan resolves it, recorded in its [levers] and load
# lines): per replay it prints the period, the kernel sum, the gaps, the host bridge (go to join, all layers) and the card's
# overlap inside it. The window a step is the period less the bridge: sum over layers of the card-only segment between a join
# and the next go. At depth 1024 the attention kernels are 5 us longer a layer than at 6 [measured, flow constants attn_seg_d6
# 10.9 us, attn_seg_d1024 16.2 us]: +0.2 ms a step, inside the band.
# Wall [derived]: V4.1 load ~10 s warm (card re-read) to 47 s cold + 1024-token prompt ~3 s + 7 replays + nsys export ~30 s +
# analysis ~20 s: 2..4 min.
kind: profile
question: how long is the card-only attention window of a V4.1 decode step, against the 11.2 ms the next layers' host bytes need
  at the union's DRAM rate?
term: the card-only window a decode step, ms (the replay's period less the host bridge go to join, all layers, mean over the 7
  timed replays of nsys-ds41.sh's decode form), V4.1 public Q3_K_M, plan (a), prose prompt P 1024, A6000
unit: ms
predict: 11..15
condition: one lease through nsys-ds41.sh with this card, BLOOMERY_MODEL=deepseek41 BLOOMERY_GEN_PLACE=a, the decode form with
  the argument prose:1024 and BLOOMERY_NSYS_N=8; the resolved residency word and the `load` line are copied into the record, and
  the per-replay table's `time step` column agrees with the period within 10 % (else the tracer stretched it and only the
  ratio of window to bridge is read); witness pre and post show no compute process on either card and no [cpu-busy]; the table
  prints 40 or 41 joins a replay (else the run is void)
decide-in: the window is 11 to 15 ms a step, at or over the 11.2 ms the next layers' reads need: the memory window exists
  and the DRAM-idle fraction stands at its derived 0.83 to 1.00; the lever's price is then h alone, so open the handoff-x dump
  (the only way to measure h; no lever exists today: a lever change, size M) before any prefetch round, and keep +10 .. +24
  percent as the upper bound at h 0.2 .. 0.4
decide-below: the window is under 11 ms: even a perfect predictor hides only window / 11.2 of the next layer's reads; scale
  the lever's price by that ratio (at 9 ms, 0.80) and write the new window into host.md 0.1 in place of 12.96
decide-above: the window is over 15 ms: the card chain is longer than the old record (a card-side regression or a deeper
  attention): the window covers the reads with room, the lever's price is unchanged, and the extra card time is a card-side
  finding to read in the same table's kernel list before any host-side round
